# HTTP Parameter Pollution (HPP) Code Examples

HTTP Parameter Pollution (HPP) is a technique where attackers send multiple parameters with the same name to exploit how servers handle duplicate parameters. This can sometimes bypass input validation and WAF rules.

## Basic HPP Examples

### 1. Simple Parameter Duplication (PHP)

```php
<?php
// Vulnerable PHP code that only checks the first parameter
$username = $_GET['user'];
if($username === 'admin') {
    echo "Access denied!";
} else {
    // Process request
    echo "Welcome, " . htmlspecialchars($username);
}
?>
```

**Attack URL:**  
`http://example.com/page.php?user=guest&user=admin`

Many PHP configurations will only see the first 'user' parameter ('guest'), while some backend systems might process the last one ('admin').

### 2. Bypassing WAF with Multiple Parameters (Node.js)

```javascript
// Express.js vulnerable endpoint
app.get('/api/user', (req, res) => {
    const id = req.query.id; // Only takes first parameter
    if(!isValidUserId(id)) {
        return res.status(400).send('Invalid ID');
    }
    // Process request
    res.send(`User data for ${id}`);
});
```

**Attack URL:**  
`http://example.com/api/user?id=legit&id=1' OR 1=1--`

The WAF might block `id=1' OR 1=1--` but allow `id=legit&id=1' OR 1=1--` if it only checks the first parameter.

### 3. Exploiting Parameter Parsing Differences (Java)

```java
